POST /api/auth/code/send
- Body: { purpose: "signup" | "login", resend: boolean }.
- First decide whether a code is needed (Rule 2). If not needed, return { required: false, reason: "trusted_device" } and send nothing, and for owner emails restore the owner claim server-side as described in Rule 2.
- If needed:
  * If an unexpired, unused code already exists and resend is false: do NOT send again. Return the current state (idempotent), so refreshes and double effects never create a second email.
  * If resend is true: enforce the cooldown. If the cooldown is still running, return 429 with { error: "cooldown", resendAvailableAt, serverTime }.
  * Otherwise create a new code, which invalidates any old one.
- Code generation: crypto.randomInt(0, 1000000), padded to 6 digits. Store only HMAC-SHA256(code, per-code random salt + CODE_HMAC_SECRET) with the salt, in Firestore collection authCodes/{uid} (server-only, rules deny all client access) with: hash, salt, purpose, createdAt, expiresAt (10 minutes), resendAvailableAt (60 seconds), attemptsLeft (5), used (false).
- Limits (enforce inside Firestore transactions so several instances agree): max 5 codes per hour per uid; max 20 sends per hour per IP; express-rate-limit burst limit on the route; 60 second cooldown between sends. Hitting a limit returns 429 with { error: "hourly_limit" | "cooldown" | "rate_limited", retryAfter, resendAvailableAt }.
- Sending: use the shared nodemailer function from Part 3 ONLY AFTER the code is stored. Return success ONLY if the provider accepted the message. If sending fails, delete or invalidate that code and return 502 { error: "send_failed" } (users see "We couldn't send the code. Try again."). Never return the code.
- Success response: { required: true, sent: true, alreadyActive: boolean, serverTime, expiresAt, resendAvailableAt, attemptsLeft } (times as epoch milliseconds).

POST /api/auth/code/verify
- Body: { code: "6 digits", trustDevice: boolean }. Validate with /^\d{6}$/.
